//- FEATURE: the home page follows the main slide sequence, then the Express appendix.
extends layout

mixin codeFiles(ids)
  p.code-map
    span.code-map-label Code to open:
    each id in ids
      a.source-link(href='/source/' + id)= sourceFiles[id].label

block content
  div.lecture-home
    header.lecture-header
      p.lecture-eyebrow INF 133 · LECTURE 9
      h1 Server-side development, authentication & authorization
      p.lecture-intro Follow the slides, show the result, then open the code that makes it happen.
      p.lecture-note Slide references use the shared PDF. Match by title if your edited Keynote numbering differs.
      nav.lecture-nav(aria-label="Lecture sections")
        a(href="#server") 1. Server-side
        a(href="#identity") 2. Identity & permissions
        a(href="#oauth") 3. OAuth
        a(href="#openid") 4. OpenID Connect
        a(href="#express") Express lab
        a(href="#files") File map

    section#server.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag MAIN LECTURE · SLIDES 7–21
      h2 1. Where is the JavaScript running?
      p.goal Show a browser making a request and a Node process sending the response.
      div.demo-entry
        h3 Node Hello World
        p Run the bundled Node example in a separate terminal from this project's root:
        pre.command npm run demo:node
        p
          a.demo-link(href="http://127.0.0.1:8080") Open Node demo · port 8080 →
        p Point out Hello, console in the terminal and Hello World in the browser. Use slides 17–18 to compare server-side and client-side execution.
        +codeFiles(['node-hello'])
      details
        summary Optional extension: read the student JSON file (slides 81–82)
        p Stop the previous Node example with Ctrl+C, then run this command from the project root. Both examples use port 8080; keep only one running there.
        pre.command npm run demo:students
        p Refresh the same Node demo URL to see the comma-separated names.
        +codeFiles(['node-starter', 'node-students', 'node-data'])
      p.transition Next: if the server can return student grades, how should it decide who may see or change them?

    section#identity.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag MAIN LECTURE · SLIDES 22–32
      h2 2. Who are you, and what may you do?
      p.goal Distinguish authentication, authorization, and multi-factor authentication before introducing third-party access.
      ol.teaching-steps
        li Use grades: a student may view their own grades; staff may have permission to update them.
        li Run the MFA PollEv question and discuss why two knowledge-based checks are not two different factors.
        li Ask: should another app receive your account password just to read some of your data?
      p.discussion-label Slides + discussion / PollEv. No separate login demo is required here.
      p.transition Next: OAuth lets a user grant limited access to a third-party application.

    section#oauth.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag MAIN LECTURE · SLIDES 33–60
      h2 3. Follow one complete OAuth flow
      p.goal Watch consent become a code, then tokens, then access to protected data.
      p
        a.demo-link(href="/oauth") Open OAuth simulation →
      p This is a local simulation with fictional accounts and data. It does not connect to Spotify.
      div.table-scroll
        table.lecture-table
          thead
            tr
              th(scope="col") Slide topic
              th(scope="col") What to do
              th(scope="col") Code to open
          tbody
            tr
              td 1. Authorize · 39–40
              td Connect → Allow access
              td
                a(href="/source/oauth-client") oauth/client.js
                br
                code GET /connect
            tr
              td Callback · 54, 69
              td Inspect the returned code and callback URL
              td
                a(href="/source/oauth-client") oauth/client.js
                br
                code GET /callback
            tr
              td 2. Get tokens · 41–42
              td Exchange code for tokens
              td
                a(href="/source/oauth-client") oauth/client.js
                br
                code POST /exchange + requestTokens()
            tr
              td 3. Call the API · 43–45
              td Request profile or playlists → 200
              td
                a(href="/source/oauth-client") oauth/client.js
                br
                code POST /api
            tr
              td 4. Refresh · 46–47
              td Expire → request → 401 → refresh → request → 200
              td
                a(href="/source/oauth-client") oauth/client.js
                br
                code POST /refresh
      p.source-hint Start with oauth/client.js. Open the mock provider only to explain how codes and tokens are issued or how scope checks work.
      +codeFiles(['oauth-client', 'oauth-provider', 'oauth-view', 'oauth-consent'])
      details
        summary Optional: deeper OAuth implementation (slides 65–79)
        p Compare the authorization query, callback code, token POST body, Basic client authentication, and Bearer header with the request log in the simulation.
        +codeFiles(['oauth-client', 'oauth-provider', 'oauth-config'])
      p.transition Ask who communicates on each arrow: browser, client backend, authorization server, or resource server.

    section#openid.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag MAIN LECTURE · SLIDES 61–63
      h2 4. Connect authorization back to identity
      p.goal Explain how OpenID Connect adds identity information for sign-in.
      p Use a “Sign in with Google” button as the example. Recap the difference between proving identity and granting access to data.
      p.discussion-label Slides + discussion. The OAuth simulation above does not implement OpenID Connect login.
      p.transition Recap the learning goals and take questions. Use the Express lab below for code details as time permits.

    section#express.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag CODE APPENDIX · SLIDES 84–100
      h2 Express lab: the implementation toolbox
      p Use these examples when you reach “More on Node and Express,” or bring a short example forward when it supports the main lecture. Expand the lab to use its controls.
      details.express-lab
        summary Open Express examples and source files
        div.lab-topic
          h3 A. A minimal Express server · slides 84–85
          p
            a(href="/hello") Hello world
            |  — a GET route and res.send().
          p The standalone file uses npm run demo:hello on port 3001. /hello on this server uses routes/index.js.
          +codeFiles(['express-hello', 'index-router'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 B. Route by HTTP method · slide 86
          p All buttons request /methods. The HTTP method selects the handler; PATCH reaches the all() fallback.
          //- FEATURE: demo.js turns data-method buttons into fetch requests.
          div.actions
            each method in ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE', 'PATCH']
              button(type="button", data-method=method)= method
          pre#method-result(aria-live="polite") Click a method to see the HTTP status and response.
          +codeFiles(['examples-router', 'browser-js'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 C. Read request data · slides 87, 99
          p
            a(href="/user/2?foo=9&course=INF133") /user/2?foo=9&course=INF133
          p Compare params.user_id with query.foo. Then compare an HTML form body with a JSON body below.
          h4 HTML form → express.urlencoded()
          //- FEATURE: native submission is parsed by express.urlencoded() in app.js.
          form(action="/echo", method="post")
            label(for="form-name") Name
            input#form-name(name="name", value="Alice", required)
            label(for="form-course") Course
            input#form-course(name="course", value="INF133", required)
            button(type="submit") Submit form
          h4 JSON request → express.json()
          //- FEATURE: demo.js intercepts this form and sends JSON with fetch().
          form#json-form
            label(for="json-name") Name
            input#json-name(name="name", value="Bob", required)
            label(for="json-course") Course
            input#json-course(name="course", value="INF133", required)
            button(type="submit") Send JSON
          pre#body-result(aria-live="polite") Send JSON to see req.body.
          +codeFiles(['users-router', 'examples-router', 'app', 'browser-js'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 D. Build a response · slide 88
          p
            a(href="/response") Write a response in parts
          p Compare status(), separate write() calls, and end() with the simpler send().
          +codeFiles(['examples-router'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 E. Middleware and cookies · slides 89–90
          p Inspect the terminal request log, then try the marker-cookie gate:
          p
            a(href="/middleware/private") Open the middleware gate
          p No cookie → 401. Set the demo cookie → next() → handler runs. This marker does not prove identity or implement a real login session.
          +codeFiles(['app', 'middleware-router'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 F. Static files · slide 91
          p
            a(href="/welcome.html") Open public/welcome.html
          p express.static() also serves the stylesheet and browser JavaScript used by this guide.
          +codeFiles(['app', 'static-html', 'browser-js'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 G. Student routes · slide 92
          ul
            li
              a(href="/students/list") /students/list — all student records
            li
              a(href="/students/2") /students/2 — one student
            li
              a(href="/students/999") /students/999 — a 404 response
          p Register /list before /:id so “list” is not interpreted as an ID.
          +codeFiles(['students-router', 'student-helper'])

        div.lab-topic
          h3 H. Routers, Pug, and project structure · slides 93–100
          p
            a(href="/students") Student HTML page generated by Pug
            = ' · '
            a(href="/api/students") The same names as JSON
            = ' · '
            a(href="/users") Mounted /users router
          p Trace app.js → router → res.render() → Pug template → HTML. Compare layout.pug with the child template's extends and block content.
          p bin/www starts the HTTP listener; app.js configures middleware and mounted routers.
          +codeFiles(['app', 'startup', 'users-router', 'students-router', 'pug-layout', 'pug-students'])

    section#files.lecture-stage
      p.slide-tag QUICK REFERENCE
      h2 Which file should I open?
      p Every link below opens the actual source. The original Node examples are bundled in examples/node so the repository works after cloning.
      details
        summary Browse all demo source files
        div.table-scroll
          table.lecture-table
            thead
              tr
                th(scope="col") File
                th(scope="col") Runs in
                th(scope="col") Feature
            tbody
              each file, id in sourceFiles
                tr
                  td
                    a(href='/source/' + id)= file.label
                  td= file.runtime
                  td= file.feature
